  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q13",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Prove that, in general, if $P(x)\\div(x-a)=Q(x)+\\dfrac{r}{x-a}$, then $P(a)=r$.\n\n(i.e. prove the remainder theorem)",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(x)=(x-a)Q(x)+r$, so $P(a)=(a-a)Q(a)+r=r$.",
      "worked": "Multiply both sides by $(x-a)$:\n$$P(x)=(x-a)Q(x)+r.$$\nSubstitute $x=a$ into this polynomial identity:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(a)&=(a-a)Q(a)+r\\\\P(a)&=r\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
